/**
@page introduction Introduction

@brief What Botanik is, how it sits next to the PLC program, and how the firmware
       is layered.

@section intro_alongside Running alongside the PLC

The Arduino OPTA carries two cores. The **M4** coprocessor runs the PLC program
written in the Axel PLC IDE: it owns the field I/O and the real-time control loop
(irrigation, ventilation, cooling). The **M7** application core runs Botanik. The
two are independent programs that share nothing but a block of memory.

Botanik is not the controller - the PLC is. Botanik is the layer the PLC program
cannot easily provide itself: a serial terminal UI, rate-limited logging, health
and watchdog supervision, and alarm notification. It reads the PLC's live state and
raised alarms over a shared-memory mailbox and presents them; it never drives the
process directly. That clean boundary is why the two halves stay separate programs
on separate cores.

@section intro_layers Three layers

The firmware is built in three layers, generic to specific, each a thin shell over the
one below.

| Layer (generic &rarr; specific) | What it is | Who writes it |
|-------|------------|---------------|
| **Base library** (`libbotanik`) | The generic, site- and target-independent machinery: the console, logger, scheduler, PLC sync, health monitor, alarm model, and the `BotanikFirmware<In, Out>` base that owns them and drives the per-tick loop. | shipped, reused as-is |
| **Site** (e.g. `gh_mlb41`) | Everything specific to one physical greenhouse and its PLC program: the shared-memory payload layout, the site pages, the alarm table, and a `GreenhouseMLB41` class deriving `BotanikFirmware`. One site, used verbatim by both the simulator and the hardware. | per greenhouse |
| **Sketch / entry point** | The thinnest layer: it selects one site and runs it. On hardware this is the Arduino sketch's `setup()`/`loop()`; on the host it is the simulator harness. | per project |

@section intro_base What the base library brings

A site author starts from a working firmware, not a blank `loop()`. `libbotanik`
already provides:

- **A non-blocking main loop** - bounded `step()`s called in a fixed, load-bearing
  order each tick (see @ref md_docs_2CONCURRENCY "the concurrency model").
- **A multiplexed console UI** - a page-based ANSI terminal with a low-bandwidth line
  editor, plus ready-made Home, Status, Logs, and Shell pages.
- **Rate-limited logging** - a dual token-bucket limiter feeding pluggable sinks, with
  undroppable criticals and edge-triggered silent-loss reporting.
- **The PLC link** - transactional, barrier-correct shared-memory RX/TX with heartbeat,
  timeout, and torn-read detection.
- **Health and supervision** - a power-on self-test, the platform-health alarm set,
  alarm notification, and a staged hardware-watchdog escalation.

A site customises this through a handful of Template-Method hooks
(`register_pages()`, `register_commands()`, `on_setup()`, `on_loop()`) plus its own
payload and alarm table; it never re-implements the loop. The next pages walk that path
end to end:

- @subpage setup - initialise a project for a new site.
- @subpage quickstart - integrate the library into an Arduino PLC IDE project.
*/
